`Board.checkWinner(lastMove)` only inspects the lines that pass through the move just played — not the whole board.

```java
Symbol checkWinner(Move lastMove) {
    Symbol s = lastMove.symbol();
    for (Line line : winChecker.linesThrough(lastMove.row(), lastMove.col())) {
        if (line.allCellsMatch(board, s)) {
            return s;
        }
    }
    return null;
}
```

`linesThrough(row, col)` is a lookup, not a scan — it returns the small subset of precomputed lines (§next slide) that include this cell. On an N x N board that's at most 4 lines, regardless of N.

`WinChecker.generateLines(n)` builds the full line set for an N x N board — called once, at game setup, never per move.

```java
List<Line> generateLines(int n) {
    List<Line> lines = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int r = 0; r < n; r++) lines.add(Line.row(r, n));
    for (int c = 0; c < n; c++) lines.add(Line.column(c, n));
    lines.add(Line.diagonal(n));
    lines.add(Line.antiDiagonal(n));
    return lines;
}
```

Swap in a K-in-a-row rule (K < N) by generating every K-length window along each row/column/diagonal instead of one full-length line — `generateLines` changes, nothing else in `WinChecker` or `Board` does.
